  "body": "It has recently come to my attention that it is possible to set a cookie with the `cookieStore` API using empty name and value strings. This is not [spec compliant](https://tools.ietf.org/html/draft-ietf-httpbis-rfc6265bis-06#section-5.3) with how most browsers parse `Set-Cookie` headers.\r\n\r\nIn Chrome, it is currently it is possible to set a cookie using the following:\r\n\r\n```javascript\r\ncookieStore.set('', '');  // Promise resolves.\r\ncookieStore.get('');  // Promise resolves cookie with empty name and value.\r\n```\r\n\r\nI have not tested in other browsers but can get back to you on that if others are interested.\r\n\r\nAs discussed [here](https://github.com/httpwg/http-extensions/issues/159#issuecomment-569218858), Safari, Firefox, and now Chrome ignore cookies set with `\"\"` or `\"=\"`. I think the CookieStore API should mirror this behavior as well. The Promise returned by `set('', '')` should either be rejected or resolve and the cookie is ignored (imho I think the former is more appropriate).\r\n\r\nThere are currently [web platform tests](https://github.com/web-platform-tests/wpt/blob/master/cookie-store/change_eventhandler_for_no_name_and_no_value.tentative.https.window.js) that exercise this behavior which I believe need to be changed as well.",
